Productivity Techniques
Professional Skills group presentation | Time Management & Productivity

This note follows the research on the cost of procrastination. It covers the two methods used in Part 6 of the presentation.

The Two-Minute Rule

James Clear's version of the rule makes a habit easier to start by reducing the first action to something that takes less than two minutes. For our group presentation, that could mean opening the shared file and writing the title of one slide. The small action gives me a clear place to begin.

The Pomodoro Technique

Francesco Cirillo's method divides work into periods of focus and planned breaks. I could spend 25 minutes drafting three points for one slide and then take a five-minute break. After four focus periods, the method calls for a longer break of 15 to 30 minutes.

How they fit together

In the presentation, I use the slide title as the first step and the draft as the goal for the focus period. Keeping both examples within one assignment shows how the methods fit into a single study session.

